\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The same-sign controls. (a)~Distance between the two extrema of $|\chi -1|$ against time for PP10 ($N=256$, solid) and MM10 ($N=128$, dashed): both pairs merge, at $t=33.6$ and $32.8$. (b)~$|\Delta X|$ of the mixed pair and the centroid displacement of every PP10 and MM10 simulation. Although gravity attracts the stars of PP10 and repels those of MM10, both pairs merge at the same time, through the attraction of their shared field, and their centroids stay below $8\times 10^{-4}$. Movies: Videos~3, \href  {https://youtu.be/jsp-Tv-f68Y}{\nolinkurl  {youtu.be/jsp-Tv-f68Y}}, and~4, \href  {https://youtu.be/_J8jfzUk-m4}{\nolinkurl  {youtu.be/_J8jfzUk-m4}}.}}{9}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:samesign}{{6}{9}{The same-sign controls. (a)~Distance between the two extrema of $|\chi -1|$ against time for PP10 ($N=256$, solid) and MM10 ($N=128$, dashed): both pairs merge, at $t=33.6$ and $32.8$. (b)~$|\Delta X|$ of the mixed pair and the centroid displacement of every PP10 and MM10 simulation. Although gravity attracts the stars of PP10 and repels those of MM10, both pairs merge at the same time, through the attraction of their shared field, and their centroids stay below $8\times 10^{-4}$. Movies: Videos~3, \href {https://youtu.be/jsp-Tv-f68Y}{\nolinkurl {youtu.be/jsp-Tv-f68Y}}, and~4, \href {https://youtu.be/_J8jfzUk-m4}{\nolinkurl {youtu.be/_J8jfzUk-m4}}}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces (a)~Change of the separation, $d(t)-d_0$, for the pairs at $d_0=10$ with $|M_-|/M_+=0.60$, 0.80, 1.00 and 1.33 (R0.60, R0.80, PM10, R1.33); all share a transient near $t\approx 50$, so the change is read at late times. (b)~$d(200)-d_0$ against $|M_-|/M_+$. The separation grows when the negative-mass star is the heavier and shrinks when it is the lighter; the change crosses zero at $|M_-|/M_+=1.00\pm 0.03$, as predicted.}}{10}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:massscale}{{7}{10}{(a)~Change of the separation, $d(t)-d_0$, for the pairs at $d_0=10$ with $|M_-|/M_+=0.60$, 0.80, 1.00 and 1.33 (R0.60, R0.80, PM10, R1.33); all share a transient near $t\approx 50$, so the change is read at late times. (b)~$d(200)-d_0$ against $|M_-|/M_+$. The separation grows when the negative-mass star is the heavier and shrinks when it is the lighter; the change crosses zero at $|M_-|/M_+=1.00\pm 0.03$, as predicted}{figure.7}{}}
